# The rows marked <-- are the ones where the ANSWER depends on the state of
# the allocator rather than on the codec.  Every one of them is an encode
# whose output is larger than anything the process had allocated BEFORE it:
# P1.2 at 218 KiB, which is the first thing past the 128 KiB threshold, and
# P2.4 at 979 KiB, which is the first thing past what P1.2 raised it to.
# P2.2 and P2.3 sit between the two and are clean, because by the time they
# run glibc has already adjusted -- which is the whole point: the figure
# depends on WHAT RAN BEFORE IT, so it is a property of the case list and not
# of the codec.  Every decode is clean at every size, because a decode
# allocates many small objects rather than one large buffer.
